    "C67","C66","C75","C80","C82","C84","C85","C86"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct human absolute or estimated oral bioavailability values for the most transferable steroid contexts are below 20%: testosterone 2–8% (C01), abiraterone acetate <10% fasted (C07, C14, C20), testosterone undecanoate 6.8 ± 3.3% (C10, C22), thienorphine very low (C15), mometasone <1% (C09), and fulvestrant low/poor (C04, C16, C18, C19). These support F < 20% for the query's steroid-phenol scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C07","C14","C20","C10","C22","C15","C09","C04","C16","C18","C19"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall evidence shows presystemic loss for structurally related steroid phenols: estradiol undergoes initial intestinal metabolism to glucuronide/sulfate conjugates (C67); ethynyl estradiol is a P-gp substrate with efflux ratio ~11 collapsing to 1.2 with inhibitor (C66); estriol shows P-gp-mediated efflux (ER 3.2 to 1.3 with inhibitor, C73; ER ~1 with GF120918, C72).",
      "card_ids": ["C67","C66","C73","C72"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability records show substantial hepatic metabolism for estradiol and related steroid phenols: rat liver S9 CLint 175–182 µL/min/mg (C75, C76), human liver microsomal glucuronidation CLint 135.57 µL/min/mg (C80), estradiol 2-hydroxylation CLint 7.5 µL/min/mg (C84), CYP3A4/5-mediated 2-/4-hydroxylation (C82, C86), P450 dependence (C85), and UGT1A10/UGT1A1 conjugation of ent-estradiol/epiestradiol (C79, C83). This supports extensive hepatic first-pass loss.",
      "card_ids": ["C75","C76","C79","C80","C82","C83","C84","C85","C86"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for related steroid phenols are not dose-normalized and cannot independently establish F; low pg/mL Cmax values (estradiol 13.3 pg/mL after 0.625 mg, C50; ethinylestradiol 58.5–104 pg/mL after 0.03 mg, C39/C43/C49; levonorgestrel 8.25 pg/mL, C41) do not provide affirmative evidence for F ≥ 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C50","C39","C43","C49","C41"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility records show very low aqueous solubility for related steroids (estradiol very low, C58; danazol ~0.6–10 µg/mL, C51/C53/C55/C56/C57/C61; progesterone 12 µg/mL, C62), supporting an absorption limitation; high Caco-2 permeability for testosterone (C52) does not overcome first-pass loss.",
      "card_ids": ["C58","C51","C53","C55","C56","C57","C61","C62","C52"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Positive-sounding relative or non-absolute statements (thienorphine better than buprenorphine, C24/C25; DMAU orally bioavailable, C36; Casodex 72% at 1 mg/kg in male rats, C38) and L5 high-permeability records for unrelated molecules (morphine C70, naloxone C74, glyceollin C71, THC SMEDDS C63) are different molecules/species/doses/formulations or non-absolute, and do not establish F ≥ 20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C24","C25","C36","C38","C70","C74","C71","C63"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C75","C76","C79","C80","C82","C83","C84","C85","C86"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself; the low prediction relies on transfer from structurally related steroid contexts.",
    "L6 hepatic-clearance records are for estradiol and related steroid phenols, not the query; species (rat vs human) and assay systems (liver S9, microsomes, recombinant UGTs) differ from in vivo oral absorption.",
    "In vitro intrinsic clearance does not directly quantify oral bioavailability fraction; first-pass extraction also depends on liver blood flow, intestinal metabolism, and formulation effects.",
    "No query-specific intestinal metabolism, P-gp efflux, solubility, or dissolution data are available; phenol conjugation and CYP oxidation are inferred from analogs.",
    "Some L6 records concern estrone, ent-estradiol, epiestradiol, or 2MeO-E2 rather than the query, limiting direct transferability.",
    "L3 exposure records report Cmax but not bioavailability fractions and are not dose-normalized, so they cannot independently establish F relative to the 20% threshold."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low (F < 20%) because direct human absolute or estimated oral bioavailability values for the most transferable steroid contexts are below 20% (testosterone 2–8%, abiraterone acetate <10%, testosterone undecanoate 6.8%, thienorphine very low, mometasone <1%, fulvestrant low), L5 gut-wall evidence shows intestinal metabolism and P-gp efflux for related steroid phenols, and L6 hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability records show substantial estradiol-like hepatic metabolism (rat S9 CLint ~175–182 µL/min/mg, human microsomal glucuronidation CLint ~135.6 µL/min/mg, CYP3A4/5-mediated oxidation), supporting extensive presystemic loss; no affirmative evidence supports F ≥ 20%."
}

